Narrator: *Thorinn stirs on the marble floor, the weight of a cape covering him. Yet the cold lingers, sending an uneasy shiver down his spine.*
Narrator: *He can’t make out anything at first, but slowly, his vision clears. He’s in a strange hall, one he doesn’t recognise.*
Narrator: *There are huge pillars surrounding him and in the middle is towering statue of an old, bald man.*
[signal arg="proceed_intro_background"]
Ludix: [b]Next soul[/b], time of death\: 18\:12.
Thorinn (surprised): What... where am I? What’s going on?
Ludix: I’m Iudix, [b]God of Judgment[/b]. We’ve crossed paths because your mortal life ended in tragedy.
Ludix: Now your soul will be put on the scale against [b]the feather of fate[/b].
[signal arg="proceed_intro_background"]
Narrator: *Thorinn feels a strange emptying pull. Then sees something shiny levitate from his chest towards a scale on the left of the god.*
Ludix: The scale will determine your fate, whether you will spend the rest of eternity in heaven or in hell.
Narrator: *Even if the scale tips for the shortest amount of time, it returns to a perfectly balanced state immediately.*
Narrator: *Then there was silence.*
Narrator: *The god moves for the first time since Thorinn woke up here, it seems unnatural for the statue like being to be shuffling.*
Narrator: *The god clears his throat and repeats.*
Ludix: [b]Now your soul will be put on the scale against the feather of fate[/b].
[signal arg="proceed_intro_background"]
Narrator: *A creepy silence and anticipation fill the space but the scale does not move.*
[signal arg="proceed_intro_background"]
Ludix: [i]How odd.[/i] This has never happened before. You are an intriguing case, with both your good and bad choices holding equal influence.
Thorinn (surprised): What's the meaning of that? Are you [i]serious[/i]?
Ludix: Your fate is still [b]uncertain[/b] as the scale cannot determine your destination based on your past choices.
Thorinn: Now hold on a minute. All this doesn't make [i]sense[/i].
Ludix: Well yes, this is a [b]tricky situation[/b].
Ludix: [i]What should we do... What should we do...
Ludix: I lack the authority to make a finite decission in an uncertain case.
Ludix: I shall test your character in [b]another[/b] way.
Ludix: That means there's only one way to determine the outcome.
Ludix: As it has not been clear up until now, I will remove your ability to remember your decisions of the past.
Ludix: You will then have to relive [i]some[/i] of your experiences to see what you would choose. And [b]every[/b] choice you make, will shape your fate.
Ludix: I have chosen to send you back to where your career began, in Rockfall.
Ludix: [b]Now be gone, I have more work to do.
[signal arg="proceed_into_demo"]